# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Repository execution

## Read the owning sources

Design: {{DESIGN_SOURCE}}
Development plan and acceptance: {{DEVELOPMENT_SOURCE}}
ChatGPT Project Instructions source: {{PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_SOURCE}}
Journal: {{JOURNAL_PATH}}; timezone: {{JOURNAL_TIMEZONE}}

These files own different facts. Design defines intended behavior; code and
runtime establish implementation; the journal records history. Read applicable
nested instructions before changing a directory. Preserve host/tool authority.

## Inspect before changing

Inspect Git status, staged/unstaged changes, the task's design/acceptance, and
affected code/tests. Preserve unrelated user work, including pre-staged hunks.
Discussion/review does not authorize file or journal changes. Ordinary in-scope
engineering work proceeds under existing implementation authority.

## Repository boundaries

Directory ownership and subsystem boundaries: {{DIRECTORY_BOUNDARIES}}
Generated files and local state to exclude: {{GENERATED_AND_LOCAL_FILES}}
Project-specific constraints: {{PROJECT_CONSTRAINTS}}

Use the smallest solution satisfying current requirements. Document a supported
design amendment before dependent code. Ask only about material unresolved
contract choices, not routine file layout, naming, or test details.

## Run verified commands

{{VERIFICATION_COMMANDS}}

For each command above, retain its working directory, prerequisites, and scope.
Use check mode for review. Run targeted checks after a coherent change, broaden
to affected interfaces and required gates, review findings, repair within scope,
and rerun invalidated checks. Classify failures rather than weakening tests.

## Synchronize and finish

Synchronize affected design, development status, and the dated journal for
authorized changes. Append decisions and observed evidence; do not copy chat
transcripts or overwrite prior historical entries.

Before implementation completion, map acceptance to current evidence and inspect
the final staged/unstaged/task-owned untracked diff. Missing mandatory evidence
keeps implementation partial. Read-only review finishes with findings and
coverage limits; it does not require fixing defects or accepting the subject.
Report results and limitations without claiming zero defects.
Commit, push, release, and deployment each need their corresponding authority;
one explicit request may authorize several. Verify each actual result separately.
